{%- comment -%}
  Bassface predictive search results — the fetch target for the search overlay.

  This section is never placed in a template. It exists only to be requested by
  URL, which is the Section Rendering API's contract:

    /search/suggest?q=<term>&section_id=bf-predictive-search
      &resources[type]=product,collection&resources[limit]=<n>

  bf-search.js takes the returned document, lifts #shopify-section-<id> out of
  it, and swaps the markup straight in. That is why this returns HTML and not
  JSON: the cards are already Liquid snippets, and re-implementing them in
  client-side templates would be two sources of truth for one card. Dawn's own
  predictive-search.liquid works exactly this way and stays in place until the
  stock header search is retired.

  TWO LIMITS OF THIS ENDPOINT, both of which shape the design:

  1. It returns NO total count. `search.results_count` exists on the search
     page; there is no equivalent here, so the overlay cannot say "94 results".
     What it CAN do is ask for more than it intends to show and compare — see
     the note on `shown` below.
  2. `resources[limit]` is capped at 10 per type by Shopify.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  comment
    Shown vs fetched, and they are deliberately different numbers.

    The overlay displays one row — four of each by default — because a search
    panel is a shortlist, not a results page. But it still asks for the full
    ten, because "are there more than we are showing" cannot be answered from a
    truncated set: request four, receive four, and a store with exactly four
    matches is indistinguishable from one with forty. The surplus is counted
    and discarded, and it is what lights the "show all" button.
  endcomment
  assign shown = settings.search_result_limit | default: 4

  assign products = predictive_search.resources.products
  assign collections = predictive_search.resources.collections

  assign has_products = false
  assign has_collections = false
  if products.size > 0
    assign has_products = true
  endif
  if collections.size > 0
    assign has_collections = true
  endif

  assign has_more = false
  if products.size > shown or collections.size > shown
    assign has_more = true
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  data-results-empty is read by bf-search.js to decide between the results and
  empty-state presentations. A data attribute rather than a class because it is
  information the server is reporting, not a state the client is holding.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div
  class="search__results"
  data-search-results
  data-results-empty="{% if has_products or has_collections %}false{% else %}true{% endif %}"
  data-results-more="{{ has_more }}"
>
  {%- if has_products -%}
    <section class="search__group">
      {%- comment -%}
        From theme settings rather than the locale file, so the results headings
        and the idle ones the overlay already renders cannot drift apart. Adding
        a `general.search.collections` key instead would mean editing a Dawn
        locale file — and the 30-odd translations beside it — to name two
        headings the merchant can already set.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <h2 class="search__label">{{ settings.search_products_heading }}</h2>
      <div class="search__grid search__grid--products">
        {%- for product in products limit: shown -%}
          <div class="search__cell">
            {%- render 'bf-product-card',
              product: product,
              ratio: '3 / 4',
              sizes: '(min-width: 1024px) 22vw, 45vw',
              index: forloop.index0,
              stacked: true
            -%}
          </div>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>
    </section>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- if has_collections -%}
    <section class="search__group">
      <h2 class="search__label">{{ settings.search_collections_heading }}</h2>
      <div class="search__grid search__grid--collections">
        {%- for collection in collections limit: shown -%}
          <div class="search__cell">
            {%- render 'bf-collection-card',
              collection: collection,
              ratio: '7 / 6',
              sizes: '(min-width: 1024px) 20vw, 45vw'
            -%}
          </div>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>
    </section>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- unless has_products or has_collections -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Rendered whether or not a search ran, because an empty resource set and a
      search that was never performed look identical from here. bf-search.js
      only ever mounts this markup after a real query, so the wording can assume
      one.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <p class="search__empty">
      {{ 'templates.search.no_results' | t: terms: predictive_search.terms }}
    </p>
  {%- endunless -%}
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Predictive search",
  "settings": []
}
{% endschema %}
